    {
      "name": "sha256-uppercase/encode-string EMITS lowercase (§2.5 RECOMMENDED)",
      "$comment": "LCP v0.1.38 §2.5 gained a RECOMMENDED that an atrHash is EMITTED lowercase. Emission only: the read side returns a received value verbatim, which vectors/placement/visa-tap.json pins as its own case. A caller holding an uppercase-digit spelling of a hash gets one wire form out of this encoder.",
      "input": {
        "op": "encodeString",
        "arg": {
          "type": "sha256",
          "value": "0x7F83B1657FF1FC53B92DC18148A1D65DFC2D4B1FA3D677284ADDD200126D9069"
        }
      },
      "expected": "lcp:sha256:0x7f83b1657ff1fc53b92dc18148a1d65dfc2d4b1fa3d677284addd200126d9069"
    },
    {
      "name": "url/encode-string preserves case EXACTLY — only sha256 is folded",
      "$comment": "The normalization is scoped to sha256 and that scoping is load-bearing, not tidiness. A URL's path and query are case-SENSITIVE, so lowercasing one would rewrite the reference into a different document. The same is true of the other content-addressed types: base58btc CIDs are mixed-case by construction and Arweave ids are base64url. §2.5's recommendation is about an atrHash's hex digits and nothing else.",
      "input": {
        "op": "encodeString",
        "arg": {
          "type": "url",
          "value": "https://Seller.Example/Terms?Ref=AbC"
        }
      },
      "expected": "lcp:url:https://Seller.Example/Terms?Ref=AbC"
    }
  ]
